\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Declared-seed branching of the lone throat (level 3). Areal throat radius of the drainhole ($a=2$, $m=1$; $R_\star =3.8895$, dotted) under the kick of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:seed}: $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ (heavy), $\pm 10^{-3}$ (light); dashed = outward ($\varepsilon >0$). The fate is opposite to the push, and each pair crosses itself at the $\varepsilon $-independent $t_\times \simeq 13.0$---linear response. Burgundy: exponential fits $|R-R_\star |\propto e^{t/\tau }$, $\tau =4.5$ ($t=18$--$31$) and $4.0$ ($t=16$--$26$)---steeper than the unkicked mode's $5.88$ (Fig.~\ref {fig:branches}) while the kick's transient decays. Dots: the scan loses the throat (the inflating runs continue to $t=100$, still growing); cross: death at $t=40.1$. The $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ arm holds a horizon from $t=11$ (areal radius $3.88\to 2.34\to 2.57$) and runs clean to $t=100$; the muted line carries its last reading. A tenfold smaller kick arrives $8$--$12$ units later.}}{5}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seed_branches}{{2}{5}{Declared-seed branching of the lone throat (level 3). Areal throat radius of the drainhole ($a=2$, $m=1$; $R_\star =3.8895$, dotted) under the kick of Eq.~\eqref {eq:seed}: $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ (heavy), $\pm 10^{-3}$ (light); dashed = outward ($\varepsilon >0$). The fate is opposite to the push, and each pair crosses itself at the $\varepsilon $-independent $t_\times \simeq 13.0$---linear response. Burgundy: exponential fits $|R-R_\star |\propto e^{t/\tau }$, $\tau =4.5$ ($t=18$--$31$) and $4.0$ ($t=16$--$26$)---steeper than the unkicked mode's $5.88$ (Fig.~\ref {fig:branches}) while the kick's transient decays. Dots: the scan loses the throat (the inflating runs continue to $t=100$, still growing); cross: death at $t=40.1$. The $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ arm holds a horizon from $t=11$ (areal radius $3.88\to 2.34\to 2.57$) and runs clean to $t=100$; the muted line carries its last reading. A tenfold smaller kick arrives $8$--$12$ units later}{figure.2}{}}
