\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Attraction and repulsion switched by one sign ($d=12$, released from rest). (a)~Separation change of three pairs one knob apart: the like-oriented pair ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) pushes apart; flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull; the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light) opens $2.9\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width (the scalar charge), not the mass, which is equal in all three. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the mouth tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~The flipped pair's infall over the like pair's escape: with $Q \equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}| = 5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), pull and push are $(Q{+}1)$ and $(Q{-}1)$, predicting $3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.52\pm 0.02$ over $t=3.5$--$10.5$. Coordinates under-read the motion while the gauge settles (band), but that systematic is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio: the two runs differ in exactly one parameter.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sign_rule}{{3}{6}{Attraction and repulsion switched by one sign ($d=12$, released from rest). (a)~Separation change of three pairs one knob apart: the like-oriented pair ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) pushes apart; flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull; the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light) opens $2.9\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width (the scalar charge), not the mass, which is equal in all three. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the mouth tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~The flipped pair's infall over the like pair's escape: with $Q \equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}| = 5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), pull and push are $(Q{+}1)$ and $(Q{-}1)$, predicting $3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.52\pm 0.02$ over $t=3.5$--$10.5$. Coordinates under-read the motion while the gauge settles (band), but that systematic is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio: the two runs differ in exactly one parameter}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{LastBibItem}{{40}{6}{}{section*.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Placement is on the ruler; the squeeze is not. (a)~Per-mouth areal radius of two exact drainhole throats placed at rest, read at $t=0$ by one-step probes at separations $d=6$--$48$ (ink): the pair reads wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted), the excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler, not a dynamical widening. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (the $\sigma =-1$ pair released from rest at $d=8$, Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation it had reached; open squares, here and in (b): the scout closer than the closest probe. (b)~The scout against the curve at its own separation, $\delta R/R = R_{\mathrm  {scout}}/R_{\mathrm  {place}}-1$. The $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ is the mouth tracker's grid snap ($d$ reads $7.94$ for a true $8$) and calibrates the systematic; the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$ ($d=6.1$). In the band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound---the apparent recovery is the ruler's, not the throats'. A boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze, if anything underestimated.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:placement}{{4}{7}{Placement is on the ruler; the squeeze is not. (a)~Per-mouth areal radius of two exact drainhole throats placed at rest, read at $t=0$ by one-step probes at separations $d=6$--$48$ (ink): the pair reads wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted), the excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler, not a dynamical widening. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (the $\sigma =-1$ pair released from rest at $d=8$, Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation it had reached; open squares, here and in (b): the scout closer than the closest probe. (b)~The scout against the curve at its own separation, $\delta R/R = R_{\mathrm {scout}}/R_{\mathrm {place}}-1$. The $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ is the mouth tracker's grid snap ($d$ reads $7.94$ for a true $8$) and calibrates the systematic; the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$ ($d=6.1$). In the band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound---the apparent recovery is the ruler's, not the throats'. A boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze, if anything underestimated}{figure.4}{}}
